      "source_id": "1",
      "stem": "Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "We can model a marathon runner as a point mass when tracking the runner's position over a 10 km race.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "This is a valid approximation because the runner's size is negligible compared with the total distance traveled. In large-scale translational motion, we often track only the center of mass, so a point-particle model is appropriate when the object's dimensions are much smaller than the motion scale."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "We can model a marathon runner as a point mass when analyzing the bending motion of the runner's knee.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is incorrect because knee motion depends on the runner's detailed geometry and rotation, which a point-mass model cannot represent."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "We can never imagine the runner moving in a vacuum, even if we want to ignore air resistance in a simplified model.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses an idealized model with literal reality; physicists often neglect drag by using a vacuum approximation when it is useful."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is false because option A gives a standard and appropriate modeling approximation in kinematics."
        }
      ],
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "skipped": false,
      "skip_reason": "",
      "answer": "",
      "parts": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "2",
      "stem": "Which of the following equations is correct?",
      "options": [
