    {
      "source_id": "4",
      "stem": "A rescue drone starts at the origin and detects a beacon at coordinates \\(\\vec r = 8\\hat{i}+15\\hat{j}\\), where distance is measured in kilometers. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The beacon is 23 km from the origin.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This adds the vector components directly instead of finding the magnitude of the position vector."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The beacon is 15 km from the origin.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This uses only the \\(y\\)-component and ignores the \\(x\\)-component in the distance calculation."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The beacon is 8 km from the origin.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This uses only the \\(x\\)-component instead of the full two-dimensional displacement."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The beacon is 17 km from the origin.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The distance from the origin is the magnitude of the position vector, \\(|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{8^2+15^2}\\). Evaluating gives \\(|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{64+225}=\\sqrt{289}=17\\,\\mathrm{km}\\), so this statement is correct."
        }
      ],
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "skipped": false,
      "skip_reason": "",
      "answer": "",
      "parts": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "5",
      "stem": "If the displacement of an object is given by \\(\\vec s(t)=\\left[(0.80\\,\\mathrm{m/s^3})t^3+(1.60\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2})t^2\\right]\\hat{i}\\), which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "This is motion with constant acceleration.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "A cubic position function produces a time-dependent acceleration, so the acceleration cannot be constant."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration is \\(1.60\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\).",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This mistakes a coefficient in the position function for the actual second derivative needed to find acceleration."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at \\(t=0\\) is \\(3.20\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\).",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Differentiate twice: \\(v_x=\\frac{ds_x}{dt}=2.40t^2+3.20t\\) and \\(a_x=\\frac{dv_x}{dt}=4.80t+3.20\\). At \\(t=0\\), this gives \\(a_x(0)=3.20\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), so the statement is correct."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at \\(t=0\\) is \\(0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\).",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This comes from differentiating incorrectly or assuming the acceleration must vanish at the start."
        }
      ],
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "skipped": false,
      "skip_reason": "",
      "answer": "",
      "parts": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "6",
      "stem": "An object takes 4.0 s to move from position \\(\\vec r_A = 1\\hat{i}-2\\hat{j}\\) m to position \\(\\vec r_B = 9\\hat{i}+4\\hat{j}\\) m along a straight path. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The moving distance is \\(10\\,\\mathrm{m}\\).",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Because the path is stated to be straight, the moving distance equals the magnitude of the displacement from A to B. Here \\(\\Delta \\vec r=(9-1)\\hat i+(4-(-2))\\hat j=8\\hat i+6\\hat j\\), so the distance is \\(|\\Delta \\vec r|=\\sqrt{8^2+6^2}=10\\,\\mathrm{m}\\)."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The speed at \\(t=0\\) must be \\(2.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\).",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses average speed over the trip with the instantaneous speed at the start, which is not determined from the given data."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The acceleration at \\(t=0\\) must be \\(0.625\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\).",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Distance and travel time alone do not determine the acceleration without an additional model of the motion."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is false because option A follows directly from the straight-line displacement magnitude."
        }
      ],
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "skipped": false,
      "skip_reason": "",
      "answer": "",
      "parts": []
