    {
      "source_id": "7",
      "stem": "A grasshopper launches itself with speed \\(u\\) at an angle \\(\\phi\\) above the horizontal. Neglecting air resistance, what is the speed of the grasshopper at the maximum height of its trajectory?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "\\(u\\sin\\phi\\)",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is the initial vertical component of the velocity, and that component becomes zero at the highest point."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "\\(0\\)",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This assumes the entire velocity vanishes at maximum height, ignoring the nonzero horizontal component."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "\\(u\\cos\\phi\\)",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "At maximum height, the vertical component of velocity is zero while the horizontal component remains constant when air resistance is neglected. Since the horizontal component is initially \\(u\\cos\\phi\\), the speed there is \\(\\sqrt{(u\\cos\\phi)^2+0^2}=u\\cos\\phi\\)."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "\\(u\\)",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This ignores the reduction of the vertical component due to gravity during projectile motion."
