    {
      "source_id": "8",
      "stem": "A drone is initially moving with velocity \\(\\vec v_0=(12.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s})\\hat{i}\\). Its motors then produce a constant acceleration \\(\\vec a=(1.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2})\\hat{i}+(2.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2})\\hat{j}\\). Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The \\(x\\)-component of the drone's velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This ignores the nonzero \\(x\\)-acceleration, which changes \\(v_x\\) over time."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The \\(y\\)-component of the drone's velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This ignores the nonzero \\(y\\)-acceleration, which causes \\(v_y\\) to increase from zero."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Both the direction of motion and the speed change.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The velocity evolves as \\(\\vec v(t)=\\vec v_0+\\vec a t\\). Since both \\(a_x\\neq 0\\) and \\(a_y\\neq 0\\), the velocity components change, which changes the direction; and because the magnitude \\(|\\vec v|\\) depends on those components, the speed changes as well."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is incorrect because option C correctly describes the effect of a two-dimensional acceleration on the motion."
        }
